<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/expert/social-theory-marx-to-bourdieu">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Social theory is sociology's think-engine: <strong>the frameworks that decide what counts as
    an explanation</strong>. The classic three (Marx: conflict; Durkheim: structure; Weber:
    meaning) still organise every question in the field — and the 20th-century extensions turned
    their insights into precision tools.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founders, their core moves, and the modern toolbox" widget="equationStep" config={theoryCfg} />

  <h2>Reading theory as a practitioner</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Each theory is a lens, not a verdict:</strong> a strike looks like class conflict (Marx), normative breakdown (Durkheim), or meaning-making around legitimacy (Weber). All three analyses run; each reveals what the others miss.</li>
    <li><strong>Bourdieu is the bridge to empirical work:</strong> habitus/capital/field generate testable predictions — his concepts became measurement instruments (the class lesson's cultural capital, operationalised).</li>
    <li><strong>Goffman changed everyday vocabulary:</strong> "front stage/back stage", "impression management" — micro-sociology's dramaturgical frame is now how everyone (including you on social media) unconsciously describes life.</li>
    <li><strong>Theory debates are method debates:</strong> structure vs agency, macro vs micro, positivism vs interpretivism — every research design takes sides. Learning theory is learning what your methods can see.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Marx, Capital (1867); Durkheim, Suicide (1897); Weber, The Protestant Ethic (1905); Bourdieu, Distinction (1979); Goffman, The Presentation of Self (1959); Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975).</p>
</TopicPage>
